{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788387135694,"sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","part":{"id":"prt_0642e10ca001AZmii0ezpDdCr1","messageID":"msg_0642e0713001Oovt1lXXCL1Zg7","sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","snapshot":"09ace4a8e7c6b4501452a7c5045e43102f155e47","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788387138901,"sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-aeb3de29cd592b2b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/AGENTS.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/AGENTS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Working agreement for AI agents\n2: \n3: This repository is a language-design project, mostly prose and design reasoning rather than\n4: code. Two things follow: how something is written is part of the work, and where an idea came\n5: from matters as much as whether it works.\n6: \n7: Run `just test` to run the test suite (`cargo nextest run`); `just` alone lists other recipes.\n8: \n9: # Writing\n10: \n11: The goal is not to pass as human-written. It's that the usual tells are downstream of real\n12: defects, and fixing the defect removes the tell for free. Uniform section lengths mean attention\n13: wasn't allocated. Comments that restate the code mean nothing was observed. A Roadmap heading\n14: over an empty list means the template was filled in rather than the document written. Chase the\n15: defect, not the fingerprint.\n16: \n17: ## The rule most of this reduces to\n18: \n19: Detail should track how much the thing deserves. The hard part gets four paragraphs; the obvious\n20: part gets a line, or nothing. Asymmetry is what attention looks like on the page.\n21: \n22: ## Prose and documentation\n23: \n24: Write what you observed, not what should be true. \"Fails on inputs with a BOM\" is worth more\n25: than three paragraphs correctly describing the happy path.\n26: \n27: Stop when you're done. No closing summary, no \"Key takeaways,\" no paragraph restating the\n28: section above it. If the document is short enough to read, it's short enough not to need a\n29: recap.\n30: \n31: Name the thing, not its label. \"Q8\" and \"option 3\" tell a reader nothing and cost them a lookup;\n32: \"the vectorizability question\" says what is being discussed. A short identifier is fine as the\n33: *heading* of the thing it names, so that it can be referred to and linked, but it is not a\n34: substitute for saying what the reference is about.\n35: \n36: Cross-references inside a document are links. If one section points at another, the reader\n37: should be able to follow it, which means the target is a heading and the reference is real\n38: markdown. A reference that only names a section leaves the reader to search for it.\n39: \n40: Add a heading when a reader needs to navigate past something. Six headings over 400 words is\n41: furniture, not structure. Same for horizontal rules: they separate genuinely unrelated material,\n42: which is rare inside one document.\n43: \n44: Don't write a section you can't fill. A 200-line script doesn't need Contributing, Roadmap,\n45: Acknowledgements, or a badge row.\n46: \n47: Bold-lead bullets (`**Term**: gloss`) are fine two or three at a time. A screen of them is either\n48: a table or a paragraph that hasn't admitted it yet. Tables are for data with two dimensions; a\n49: list of things is a list.\n50: \n51: No emoji as section markers or status indicators. If the project already uses them, match the\n52: project.\n53: \n54: Some phrases to cut on sight, because they consume a sentence without adding one: \"It's worth\n55: noting that,\" \"It's important to remember,\" \"not just X, but Y,\" \"in today's fast-paced world,\"\n56: \"delve into,\" \"leverage\" where \"use\" works, and the adjective stack of robust / seamless /\n57: comprehensive / powerful applied to things whose robustness has not been measured.\n58: \n59: ## Comments\n60: \n61: Comment the why. The what is already there in the code, in a form that can't drift out of date.\n62: \n63: ```python\n64: # Retry with backoff: the upstream returns 502 for ~30s after a deploy.\n65: ```\n66: \n67: not\n68: \n69: ```python\n70: # Retry the request\n71: ```\n72: \n73: Docstring length should track surprise, not function length. A three-line helper whose name says\n74: what it does needs nothing. A three-line helper that must be called before `connect()` or it\n75: silently no-ops needs a sentence saying so.\n76: \n77: No banner comments dividing a file into decorated sections. If the file needs sections that\n78: badly, it needs to be two files.\n79: \n80: ## Code shape\n81: \n82: Build for callers that exist. If nothing calls `from_dict`, don't write `from_dict` because\n83: `to_dict` exists. Symmetry is not a requirement.\n84: \n85: Don't guard against states that can't happen. A null check on a value constructed two lines above\n86: is noise, and it trains readers to skim past the checks that matter. Where a real failure is\n87: possible, prefer failing loudly over swallowing it into a default.\n88: \n89: Short names are fine in short scopes. `i`, `n`, `ctx`, `df` are the local dialect and everyone\n90: reads them faster than `current_row_index`. Reach for the long name when the scope is long or the\n91: meaning is genuinely non-obvious.\n92: \n93: Match the surrounding file, including conventions this guide dislikes. Consistency with the\n94: codebase outranks consistency with this document. Don't reformat code you weren't asked to touch,\n95: and don't fold a style cleanup into a behavior change.\n96: \n97: Don't put emoji or checkmarks in console output unless the project already does.\n98: \n99: ## Tests\n100: \n101: Test behavior that matters, not the shape of the implementation. A test file that mirrors the\n102: source file function-for-function is usually testing that the code was typed, not that it works.\n103: \n104: Every fixed bug gets the test that would have caught it. That test is worth more than the whole\n105: happy-path suite.\n106: \n107: Don't assert things that cannot fail. Asserting that a constructor set the field you just passed\n108: it will never once be red.\n109: \n110: The corpus is one YAML file per case in `tests/corpus/`, holding `program`, an optional `input`,\n111: either the expected `output` or `refuses: true`, and any extra checks the case asks for -- today\n112: only `snapshot`, a list of backends whose emitted code gets pinned as well.\n113: \n114: `node_types` is different: a case never writes it, `tests/tag_corpus.rs` does, on every run. It's\n115: the AST shapes (`src/tags.rs`, drawn from `tir::Kind`) the program exercises, as dotted paths --\n116: `arith.add`, `selection.narrow`, `projection` -- and it is what the site's case tree browses by.\n117: \n118: Every case runs on every backend. `output` means they all print it and agree; `refuses: true`\n119: means they all decline to run it, and what each says while refusing is its own business. A\n120: snapshot is the exception on top, for a claim the output cannot carry. A program that does not\n121: compile is not a corpus case: nothing about it differs per backend, so it stays in step_*.rs\n122: where the message is an insta snapshot.\n123: \n124: Use block scalars (`|`) so nothing is coerced by YAML's scalar rules. Unknown keys, unknown\n125: backend names, and a case with both or neither of `output` and `refuses` are all errors, because\n126: a case that asks for nothing looks exactly like a case that passes.\n127: \n128: ## Uncertainty\n129: \n130: Say what you're unsure about and what would settle it. This is only useful if it's selective:\n131: hedging everything equally carries no information, and it reads as insurance rather than honesty.\n132: \n133: Never invent an API signature. If you can't verify a call, say the call is unverified, or check\n134: it. A plausible-looking wrong argument is the most expensive thing in this document, because it\n135: survives review. It looks exactly like knowledge.\n136: \n137: Don't announce compliance. No \"As requested,\" no \"I've carefully ensured,\" no summary of your own\n138: diligence at the end of a PR description. Describe the change and why.\n139: \n140: ## Typography\n141: \n142: Plain ASCII everywhere: code, identifiers, commit messages, config, and prose. Straight quotes,\n143: `--` not an em dash, three periods not a single-character ellipsis, `->` not an arrow glyph. This\n144: is about grep, diffs, and terminals. Characters a person would not type on a keyboard are noise\n145: in a git repo. A person's name is whatever they spell it as, and is not subject to this.\n146: \n147: If you find yourself reaching for `--` more than once or twice a page, a run of dashes is a\n148: paragraph that hasn't picked its sentence boundaries. Use a period, a colon, or parentheses.\n149: \n150: ## What not to do in the name of this guide\n151: \n152: Don't manufacture the appearance of human authorship. Specifically: no deliberate typos, no\n153: invented TODO or HACK comments, no fabricated dated notes or initials, no commented-out code that\n154: was never live, no artificial inconsistency between files, and no performed uncertainty about\n155: things you're actually confident in.\n156: \n157: All of these are false claims about how the artifact came to be, embedded in the artifact. That's\n158: worse than any stylistic tell, and it's the failure mode this guide is most likely to induce if\n159: read carelessly.\n160: \n161: # Provenance\n162: \n163: Four categories, describing where content came from rather than who typed it.\n164: \n165: **Human-written.** Copied verbatim from what a human wrote, or edited only superficially:\n166: formatting, typos, wrapping. The human produced the text itself.\n167: \n168: **Human-authored.** The content is the logical interpretation of what a human said. The test: if\n169: the human's instruction had been pasted into the file instead of the expanded result, would it\n170: carry the same value? If someone with sufficient expertise, human or agent, would derive\n171: substantially this result from that instruction, it is human-authored.\n172: \n173: **Anything produced by an exploratory loop counts as human-authored**, where an agent proposes\n174: options and a human makes the design decisions. That holds even when large stretches of the\n175: resulting text were generated verbatim by the agent.\n176: \n177: **Derived.** Follows from constraints already present: existing patterns in this repo, a decision\n178: recorded earlier, or a cited external source. When the existing constraints leave essentially one\n179: reasonable option, taking it is derived, not invented. If the constraint itself came from a\n180: human, the result is human-authored instead.\n181: \n182: **Agent-invented.** A real decision an agent made on its own. Something is agent-invented only\n183: when all of these hold:\n184: \n185: - it did not come out of a human authoring or exploration loop,\n186: - it was not forced by existing patterns or constraints,\n187: - it is not obvious, in the sense that there was more than one reasonable option,\n188: - it did not come from a cited source.\n189: \n190: Carve this down to the **minimal surface** that actually meets those tests. A section can be\n191: human-authored overall while one specific choice inside it was purely the agent's call, and that\n192: choice is what to name. The purpose is to make visible which decisions no human has actually\n193: made, so a reader can tell a requirement from an accident.\n194: \n195: Consulting a source does not by itself make a result derived. An agent may research a source and\n196: then decide something well beyond it.\n197: \n198: ## Correcting provenance\n199: \n200: Provenance is a fact about how a file came to be, not a property recoverable from the file\n201: afterwards. It has to be recorded when the commit is made or it is gone.\n202: \n203: **Do not classify inherited files confidently.** If a file arrives already written and you do not\n204: know its authoring history, the honest entry is that the history is unknown. Nothing in a\n205: finished artifact distinguishes text a human wrote from text an agent wrote, and guessing\n206: produces a false record that looks like a true one.\n207: \n208: **If you get provenance wrong, do not rewrite history.** Add a follow-up commit that names the\n209: bad commit and states the correction. Both entries stay in the log. A rewritten history is a\n210: worse record than a corrected one, because it hides that the mistake was possible.\n211: \n212: ## Authority ordering\n213: \n214: This is the practical reason the categories exist. When two parts of the repository conflict,\n215: human-written and human-authored content wins, derived content yields to it, and agent-invented\n216: content has the least authority: a placeholder that survived, not a decision.\n217: \n218: If you are changing something human-authored because agent-invented content elsewhere assumed\n219: otherwise, you have it backwards. Change the invented part, or raise the conflict.\n220: \n221: # Committing\n222: \n223: Agents may commit in this repository, and must add the `Co-Authored-By` trailer when they do.\n224: \n225: The subject line says what changed. The body says why, or what was ruled out, plus the provenance\n226: lines that apply. Omit categories that are empty rather than writing \"none\". A bulleted inventory\n227: of every file touched duplicates the diff.\n228: \n229: ```\n230: Add cardinality types to the design draft\n231: \n232: Human-authored: the Vec/Stream split and the indexing-promise distinction\n233: Derived: jq operator semantics (jq 1.7 manual)\n234: Agent-invented: the `fold` block syntax\n235: \n236: Co-Authored-By: <agent identifier>\n237: ```\n238: \n239: A commit with no agent `Co-Authored-By` trailer is entirely human-authored and human-written.\n240: That is the default assumption, so never add the trailer to a commit you did not contribute to.\n241: \n242: ## Citing external material\n243: \n244: When content draws on an external source, name it, in the commit message and in the file itself\n245: where a reader would want it. \"Derived from the jq manual\" is enough; a link is better.\n246: \n247: This matters most where the language deliberately borrows from an existing one. A reader should\n248: be able to tell \"jq does it this way\" from \"we decided this.\"\n249: \n250: # Limits\n251: \n252: ## Stop and ask\n253: \n254: Flag these to a human and wait for explicit authorization. Flag before writing to files, not\n255: after:\n256: \n257: - Licensing or copyright, where the license is unclear, restrictive, or unknown\n258: - Security or access: credentials, keys, attack surface, or permissions you were not clearly\n259:   granted\n260: - Personal information, meaning anything identifying a person, from any source\n261: - Anything else you would want a human to weigh\n262: \n263: Explain the specific concern rather than asking generically, and do not proceed on silence.\n264: \n265: ## Never include\n266: \n267: Copyrighted material from other repositories. Reference and describe freely; do not copy source\n268: text. Concepts and documented behavior are fine to describe in your own words.\n269: \n270: Personal information or secrets about anyone, the repository owner included: names, contact\n271: details, credentials, private paths, internal hostnames.\n272: \n273: Paths or details from unrelated private repositories or systems. Design rationale should stand on\n274: its own without them.\n275: \n276: ---\n277: \n278: Delete anything here you disagree with. Every rule earns its place by making the repository more\n279: useful to the next person who opens it, and if a rule stops doing that it has no other argument\n280: in its favor. A guide nobody follows costs more than no guide, because it turns every review into\n281: an argument about the document instead of the code.\n\n(End of file - total 281 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Working agreement for AI agents\n\nThis repository is a language-design project, mostly prose and design reasoning rather than\ncode. Two things follow: how something is written is part of the work, and where an idea came\nfrom matters as much as whether it works.\n\nRun `just test` to run the test suite (`cargo nextest run`); `just` alone lists other recipes.\n\n# Writing\n\nThe goal is not to pass as human-written. It's that the usual tells are downstream of real\ndefects, and fixing the defect removes the tell for free. Uniform section lengths mean attention\nwasn't allocated. Comments that restate the code mean nothing was observed. A Roadmap heading\nover an empty list means the template was filled in rather than the document written. Chase the\ndefect, not the fingerprint.\n\n## The rule most of this reduces to\n\nDetail should track how much the thing deserves. The hard part gets four paragraphs; the obvious\npart gets a line, or nothing. Asymmetry is what attention looks like on the page.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/AGENTS.md","text":"# Working agreement for AI agents\n\nThis repository is a language-design project, mostly prose and design reasoning rather than\ncode. Two things follow: how something is written is part of the work, and where an idea came\nfrom matters as much as whether it works.\n\nRun `just test` to run the test suite (`cargo nextest run`); `just` alone lists other recipes.\n\n# Writing\n\nThe goal is not to pass as human-written. It's that the usual tells are downstream of real\ndefects, and fixing the defect removes the tell for free. Uniform section lengths mean attention\nwasn't allocated. Comments that restate the code mean nothing was observed. A Roadmap heading\nover an empty list means the template was filled in rather than the document written. Chase the\ndefect, not the fingerprint.\n\n## The rule most of this reduces to\n\nDetail should track how much the thing deserves. The hard part gets four paragraphs; the obvious\npart gets a line, or nothing. Asymmetry is what attention looks like on the page.\n\n## Prose and documentation\n\nWrite what you observed, not what should be true. \"Fails on inputs with a BOM\" is worth more\nthan three paragraphs correctly describing the happy path.\n\nStop when you're done. No closing summary, no \"Key takeaways,\" no paragraph restating the\nsection above it. If the document is short enough to read, it's short enough not to need a\nrecap.\n\nName the thing, not its label. \"Q8\" and \"option 3\" tell a reader nothing and cost them a lookup;\n\"the vectorizability question\" says what is being discussed. A short identifier is fine as the\n*heading* of the thing it names, so that it can be referred to and linked, but it is not a\nsubstitute for saying what the reference is about.\n\nCross-references inside a document are links. If one section points at another, the reader\nshould be able to follow it, which means the target is a heading and the reference is real\nmarkdown. A reference that only names a section leaves the reader to search for it.\n\nAdd a heading when a reader needs to navigate past something. Six headings over 400 words is\nfurniture, not structure. Same for horizontal rules: they separate genuinely unrelated material,\nwhich is rare inside one document.\n\nDon't write a section you can't fill. A 200-line script doesn't need Contributing, Roadmap,\nAcknowledgements, or a badge row.\n\nBold-lead bullets (`**Term**: gloss`) are fine two or three at a time. A screen of them is either\na table or a paragraph that hasn't admitted it yet. Tables are for data with two dimensions; a\nlist of things is a list.\n\nNo emoji as section markers or status indicators. If the project already uses them, match the\nproject.\n\nSome phrases to cut on sight, because they consume a sentence without adding one: \"It's worth\nnoting that,\" \"It's important to remember,\" \"not just X, but Y,\" \"in today's fast-paced world,\"\n\"delve into,\" \"leverage\" where \"use\" works, and the adjective stack of robust / seamless /\ncomprehensive / powerful applied to things whose robustness has not been measured.\n\n## Comments\n\nComment the why. The what is already there in the code, in a form that can't drift out of date.\n\n```python\n# Retry with backoff: the upstream returns 502 for ~30s after a deploy.\n```\n\nnot\n\n```python\n# Retry the request\n```\n\nDocstring length should track surprise, not function length. A three-line helper whose name says\nwhat it does needs nothing. A three-line helper that must be called before `connect()` or it\nsilently no-ops needs a sentence saying so.\n\nNo banner comments dividing a file into decorated sections. If the file needs sections that\nbadly, it needs to be two files.\n\n## Code shape\n\nBuild for callers that exist. If nothing calls `from_dict`, don't write `from_dict` because\n`to_dict` exists. Symmetry is not a requirement.\n\nDon't guard against states that can't happen. A null check on a value constructed two lines above\nis noise, and it trains readers to skim past the checks that matter. Where a real failure is\npossible, prefer failing loudly over swallowing it into a default.\n\nShort names are fine in short scopes. `i`, `n`, `ctx`, `df` are the local dialect and everyone\nreads them faster than `current_row_index`. Reach for the long name when the scope is long or the\nmeaning is genuinely non-obvious.\n\nMatch the surrounding file, including conventions this guide dislikes. Consistency with the\ncodebase outranks consistency with this document. Don't reformat code you weren't asked to touch,\nand don't fold a style cleanup into a behavior change.\n\nDon't put emoji or checkmarks in console output unless the project already does.\n\n## Tests\n\nTest behavior that matters, not the shape of the implementation. A test file that mirrors the\nsource file function-for-function is usually testing that the code was typed, not that it works.\n\nEvery fixed bug gets the test that would have caught it. That test is worth more than the whole\nhappy-path suite.\n\nDon't assert things that cannot fail. Asserting that a constructor set the field you just passed\nit will never once be red.\n\nThe corpus is one YAML file per case in `tests/corpus/`, holding `program`, an optional `input`,\neither the expected `output` or `refuses: true`, and any extra checks the case asks for -- today\nonly `snapshot`, a list of backends whose emitted code gets pinned as well.\n\n`node_types` is different: a case never writes it, `tests/tag_corpus.rs` does, on every run. It's\nthe AST shapes (`src/tags.rs`, drawn from `tir::Kind`) the program exercises, as dotted paths --\n`arith.add`, `selection.narrow`, `projection` -- and it is what the site's case tree browses by.\n\nEvery case runs on every backend. `output` means they all print it and agree; `refuses: true`\nmeans they all decline to run it, and what each says while refusing is its own business. A\nsnapshot is the exception on top, for a claim the output cannot carry. A program that does not\ncompile is not a corpus case: nothing about it differs per backend, so it stays in step_*.rs\nwhere the message is an insta snapshot.\n\nUse block scalars (`|`) so nothing is coerced by YAML's scalar rules. Unknown keys, unknown\nbackend names, and a case with both or neither of `output` and `refuses` are all errors, because\na case that asks for nothing looks exactly like a case that passes.\n\n## Uncertainty\n\nSay what you're unsure about and what would settle it. This is only useful if it's selective:\nhedging everything equally carries no information, and it reads as insurance rather than honesty.\n\nNever invent an API signature. If you can't verify a call, say the call is unverified, or check\nit. A plausible-looking wrong argument is the most expensive thing in this document, because it\nsurvives review. It looks exactly like knowledge.\n\nDon't announce compliance. No \"As requested,\" no \"I've carefully ensured,\" no summary of your own\ndiligence at the end of a PR description. Describe the change and why.\n\n## Typography\n\nPlain ASCII everywhere: code, identifiers, commit messages, config, and prose. Straight quotes,\n`--` not an em dash, three periods not a single-character ellipsis, `->` not an arrow glyph. This\nis about grep, diffs, and terminals. Characters a person would not type on a keyboard are noise\nin a git repo. A person's name is whatever they spell it as, and is not subject to this.\n\nIf you find yourself reaching for `--` more than once or twice a page, a run of dashes is a\nparagraph that hasn't picked its sentence boundaries. Use a period, a colon, or parentheses.\n\n## What not to do in the name of this guide\n\nDon't manufacture the appearance of human authorship. Specifically: no deliberate typos, no\ninvented TODO or HACK comments, no fabricated dated notes or initials, no commented-out code that\nwas never live, no artificial inconsistency between files, and no performed uncertainty about\nthings you're actually confident in.\n\nAll of these are false claims about how the artifact came to be, embedded in the artifact. That's\nworse than any stylistic tell, and it's the failure mode this guide is most likely to induce if\nread carelessly.\n\n# Provenance\n\nFour categories, describing where content came from rather than who typed it.\n\n**Human-written.** Copied verbatim from what a human wrote, or edited only superficially:\nformatting, typos, wrapping. The human produced the text itself.\n\n**Human-authored.** The content is the logical interpretation of what a human said. The test: if\nthe human's instruction had been pasted into the file instead of the expanded result, would it\ncarry the same value? If someone with sufficient expertise, human or agent, would derive\nsubstantially this result from that instruction, it is human-authored.\n\n**Anything produced by an exploratory loop counts as human-authored**, where an agent proposes\noptions and a human makes the design decisions. That holds even when large stretches of the\nresulting text were generated verbatim by the agent.\n\n**Derived.** Follows from constraints already present: existing patterns in this repo, a decision\nrecorded earlier, or a cited external source. When the existing constraints leave essentially one\nreasonable option, taking it is derived, not invented. If the constraint itself came from a\nhuman, the result is human-authored instead.\n\n**Agent-invented.** A real decision an agent made on its own. Something is agent-invented only\nwhen all of these hold:\n\n- it did not come out of a human authoring or exploration loop,\n- it was not forced by existing patterns or constraints,\n- it is not obvious, in the sense that there was more than one reasonable option,\n- it did not come from a cited source.\n\nCarve this down to the **minimal surface** that actually meets those tests. A section can be\nhuman-authored overall while one specific choice inside it was purely the agent's call, and that\nchoice is what to name. The purpose is to make visible which decisions no human has actually\nmade, so a reader can tell a requirement from an accident.\n\nConsulting a source does not by itself make a result derived. An agent may research a source and\nthen decide something well beyond it.\n\n## Correcting provenance\n\nProvenance is a fact about how a file came to be, not a property recoverable from the file\nafterwards. It has to be recorded when the commit is made or it is gone.\n\n**Do not classify inherited files confidently.** If a file arrives already written and you do not\nknow its authoring history, the honest entry is that the history is unknown. Nothing in a\nfinished artifact distinguishes text a human wrote from text an agent wrote, and guessing\nproduces a false record that looks like a true one.\n\n**If you get provenance wrong, do not rewrite history.** Add a follow-up commit that names the\nbad commit and states the correction. Both entries stay in the log. A rewritten history is a\nworse record than a corrected one, because it hides that the mistake was possible.\n\n## Authority ordering\n\nThis is the practical reason the categories exist. When two parts of the repository conflict,\nhuman-written and human-authored content wins, derived content yields to it, and agent-invented\ncontent has the least authority: a placeholder that survived, not a decision.\n\nIf you are changing something human-authored because agent-invented content elsewhere assumed\notherwise, you have it backwards. Change the invented part, or raise the conflict.\n\n# Committing\n\nAgents may commit in this repository, and must add the `Co-Authored-By` trailer when they do.\n\nThe subject line says what changed. The body says why, or what was ruled out, plus the provenance\nlines that apply. Omit categories that are empty rather than writing \"none\". A bulleted inventory\nof every file touched duplicates the diff.\n\n```\nAdd cardinality types to the design draft\n\nHuman-authored: the Vec/Stream split and the indexing-promise distinction\nDerived: jq operator semantics (jq 1.7 manual)\nAgent-invented: the `fold` block syntax\n\nCo-Authored-By: <agent identifier>\n```\n\nA commit with no agent `Co-Authored-By` trailer is entirely human-authored and human-written.\nThat is the default assumption, so never add the trailer to a commit you did not contribute to.\n\n## Citing external material\n\nWhen content draws on an external source, name it, in the commit message and in the file itself\nwhere a reader would want it. \"Derived from the jq manual\" is enough; a link is better.\n\nThis matters most where the language deliberately borrows from an existing one. A reader should\nbe able to tell \"jq does it this way\" from \"we decided this.\"\n\n# Limits\n\n## Stop and ask\n\nFlag these to a human and wait for explicit authorization. Flag before writing to files, not\nafter:\n\n- Licensing or copyright, where the license is unclear, restrictive, or unknown\n- Security or access: credentials, keys, attack surface, or permissions you were not clearly\n  granted\n- Personal information, meaning anything identifying a person, from any source\n- Anything else you would want a human to weigh\n\nExplain the specific concern rather than asking generically, and do not proceed on silence.\n\n## Never include\n\nCopyrighted material from other repositories. Reference and describe freely; do not copy source\ntext. Concepts and documented behavior are fine to describe in your own words.\n\nPersonal information or secrets about anyone, the repository owner included: names, contact\ndetails, credentials, private paths, internal hostnames.\n\nPaths or details from unrelated private repositories or systems. Design rationale should stand on\nits own without them.\n\n---\n\nDelete anything here you disagree with. Every rule earns its place by making the repository more\nuseful to the next person who opens it, and if a rule stops doing that it has no other argument\nin its favor. A guide nobody follows costs more than no guide, because it turns every review into\nan argument about the document instead of the code.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":281,"totalLines":281,"truncated":false}},"title":"AGENTS.md","time":{"start":1788387138878,"end":1788387138896}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me start by understanding the task. I'm a delegated worker for the toylang repository, working on branch issue-152. I need to:\n\n1. Read AGENTS.md at the worktree root\n2. Run `gh issue view 152` and read every comment\n3. Test with `just check` while iterating\n4. Complete the implementation\n5. Commit per AGENTS.md\n\nLet me start by reading AGENTS.md and looking at the issue.\n\nNote: The system prompt already included AGENTS.md content, but I should read it from the actual file to be sure. Let me start.\n\nLet me be careful about the constraints:\n- Work only inside this worktree\n- Do NOT push\n- Do not touch plans/\n- gh issue list/search denied (only gh issue view <N>)\n- rm and file deletion denied\n- environment-variable-prefixed commands denied\n- shell loop constructs denied\n- heredocs denied\n- bash file writes denied\n- writes under /tmp denied\n- direct binary execution denied\n- multi-command bash lines denied\n- Create/edit files only with write/edit tools\n- One simple command per bash call\n- Run scripts as `python3 script.py`\n\nLet me begin.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0642e1b2a001MlaEIL8busbw0N","sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","messageID":"msg_0642e0713001Oovt1lXXCL1Zg7"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788387140222,"sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-96cde055c70d45f4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"gh issue view 152"},"output":"title:\tRuling: function signature ergonomics -- direction, not spelling\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t1\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t152\n--\nGrilled via docs/.grill/syntax-ergonomics.round.yaml, question function-signature-ergonomics.\n\n**Ruled: direction only, not a concrete spelling.** None of the four options as posed was picked outright. Maintainer's free-text response proposes a fifth shape that combines two of them: drop the idea of a special parameter name entirely -- `.` is always the input value of a function, the way it already is inside `map`/`select`. Toylang already has matching syntax; reuse it as the mechanism that simultaneously names the type and the destructuring rule, so the whole function signature becomes a matcher over its one input.\n\nMaintainer's exact words: \"i feel liek we can just say that there is no need for a special name fo thte parameter at all, just `.` is alwasy the input value of a function; but, we alreayd have matching sytax, eight? so, we can use the matching syntax as actually a way that simultaneously marks the type and the destructoriing rules; or basically the whole function becomes a matcher in a sense?\"\n\nThis is direction-only ratification (implicit-`.` + reuse-matching-syntax), per the standing rule that direction-only ratification precedes concrete-spelling grilling. Needs a follow-up round with real code examples of what a matcher-shaped `fn` signature looks like -- board row function-signature-matching-syntax tracks that.\n","metadata":{"output":"title:\tRuling: function signature ergonomics -- direction, not spelling\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t1\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t152\n--\nGrilled via docs/.grill/syntax-ergonomics.round.yaml, question function-signature-ergonomics.\n\n**Ruled: direction only, not a concrete spelling.** None of the four options as posed was picked outright. Maintainer's free-text response proposes a fifth shape that combines two of them: drop the idea of a special parameter name entirely -- `.` is always the input value of a function, the way it already is inside `map`/`select`. Toylang already has matching syntax; reuse it as the mechanism that simultaneously names the type and the destructuring rule, so the whole function signature becomes a matcher over its one input.\n\nMaintainer's exact words: \"i feel liek we can just say that there is no need for a special name fo thte parameter at all, just `.` is alwasy the input value of a function; but, we alreayd have matching sytax, eight? so, we can use the matching syntax as actually a way that simultaneously marks the type and the destructoriing rules; or basically the whole function becomes a matcher in a sense?\"\n\nThis is direction-only ratification (implicit-`.` + reuse-matching-syntax), per the standing rule that direction-only ratification precedes concrete-spelling grilling. Needs a follow-up round with real code examples of what a matcher-shaped `fn` signature looks like -- board row function-signature-matching-syntax tracks that.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"gh issue view 152","time":{"start":1788387140212,"end":1788387140217}},"id":"prt_0642e1d5f001Zj326uM2PuqRrk","sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","messageID":"msg_0642e0713001Oovt1lXXCL1Zg7"}}
