00:12 backend_js.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  2463 Sep  3 00:12 backend_llvm.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  1418 Sep  3 00:12 backend_py.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  2148 Sep  3 00:12 backend_rust.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  4110 Sep  3 00:12 bare_application.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  1322 Sep  3 00:12 char.rs\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  9604 Sep  3 00:12 corpus\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  3123 Sep  3 00:12 corpus.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord 15097 Sep  3 00:12 docs.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  7140 Sep  3 00:12 enums.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  9900 Sep  3 00:12 euler_real_data.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  3035 Sep  3 00:12 export_site.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  5107 Sep  3 00:12 fmt_examples.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  7485 Sep  3 00:12 fmt_project.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  5963 Sep  3 00:12 fmt.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  4704 Sep  3 00:12 generics.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  4814 Sep  3 00:12 int64.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  1225 Sep  3 00:12 jsonlines.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  2644 Sep  3 00:12 matcher.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  3665 Sep  3 00:12 opt.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  1653 Sep  3 00:12 reductions.rs\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord 22152 Sep  3 00:12 snapshots\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  1149 Sep  3 00:12 step_1.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  1023 Sep  3 00:12 step_2.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  3147 Sep  3 00:12 step_3.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  3450 Sep  3 00:12 step_4.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  4494 Sep  3 00:12 step_5.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  1788 Sep  3 00:12 step_dsv.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord 13482 Sep  3 00:12 streaming.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord 12289 Sep  3 00:12 streams.rs\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord    12 Sep  3 00:12 support\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  1527 Sep  3 00:12 tag_corpus.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord 11359 Sep  3 00:12 type_flow.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  2353 Sep  3 00:12 unused.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  5800 Sep  3 00:12 unwrap.rs\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord  1789 Sep  3 00:12 variant_casing.rs\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls -la src tests","time":{"start":1788387202733,"end":1788387202735}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the board row. The row `function-signature-matching-syntax` (issue gh:152) says:\n\n\"Round 5 RULED: maintainer picked A -- call-form hoist (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or Quit -> ...)), against the round-5 thesis which favored C on parse-today grounds. Maintainer note: ... Build task: implement A. gh:152 to be closed on landing.\"\n\nkind: build, status: delegated.\n\nSo my task is to implement option A: call-form hoist. The concrete example from the issue: `fn render = Msg(Ping -> \"*ping*\" or Quit -> \"*quit*\" or Text -> .body)`.\n\nLet me understand the language and how functions are currently declared. Let me read CONTEXT.md, README, and explore the source code, particularly the parser and the `fn` declaration.\n\nLet me first understand the current state. Let me look at the draft.md for function declaration syntax, and the source code.\n\nLet me explore the src directory.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0642f1537001Y6cTM7c218Dqyq","sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","messageID":"msg_0642f052a00187qrNcV5erqXLR"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788387203027,"sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b33b4b9e934b56e2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/CONTEXT.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/CONTEXT.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # toylang\n2: \n3: A compiled, statically typed language for transforming data, taking jq as its main inspiration\n4: without aiming at compatibility with it. This file is the glossary and nothing else: what the\n5: terms mean, not how anything is built. The design lives in `draft.md`, the build order in\n6: `plans/`, and what building it taught us in `research-log/`.\n7: \n8: ## Language\n9: \n10: ### Shape of a value\n11: \n12: **Scalar**:\n13: A value with no interior to address: `Int`, `Str`, `Bool`.\n14: \n15: **Record**:\n16: A fixed set of differently-typed parts addressed by name, where the names are part of the type.\n17: Type theory calls this a product, which is exact and says nothing to a reader who has not met the\n18: word, so that name is not used here.\n19: _Avoid_: object, struct, row, product\n20: \n21: **Dimension**:\n22: An axis along which a value repeats, addressed by position, whose entries all have the same type.\n23: A `Vec` has one; a tensor has several. A type's dimensions are fixed by the type, in order.\n24: _Avoid_: axis, rank, column\n25: \n26: **Extent**:\n27: How many entries a dimension has. A number, not a type.\n28: _Avoid_: size, cardinality\n29: \n30: **Field**:\n31: One named part of a record. `name` is a field of `{name: Str, age: Int}`.\n32: _Avoid_: column, attribute, component\n33: \n34: **Map**:\n35: A collection whose keys are data rather than type-level, with one value type. Distinct from a\n36: record: a record's keys are known to the compiler, a map's are known only to the program.\n37: _Avoid_: object, dict, record\n38: \n39: **Name casing**:\n40: A name beginning with a capital letter is a type; a name beginning with a lowercase letter is a\n41: value, which covers functions and their parameters. Field names are exempt, because they come\n42: from data and a JSON object is entitled to a key spelled `Name`.\n43: \n44: ### Building a value\n45: \n46: **Record literal**:\n47: The form that builds a record from its fields, and the inverse of a projection.\n48: `{name: .n, age: .a}` is one. Its type is the names and types of its fields, so it answers what\n49: it is from its contents alone and never needs its position to say. In argument position it is\n50: also how a function takes more than one thing, because a function takes one argument and a record\n51: is how several travel as one.\n52: _Avoid_: object literal, struct literal, product literal, construction\n53: \n54: **Vec literal**:\n55: The form that builds a dimension from its entries. `[1, 2]` is one. Unlike a record literal it\n56: cannot always answer what it is, because an entry is where an element type comes from and an\n57: empty one has none.\n58: _Avoid_: array literal, list literal\n59: \n60: **Application**:\n61: Calling a function with its one argument. Two spellings, one meaning: `f x`, the bare form, is\n62: the default, and `f(x)` is the same call with the argument grouped -- the disambiguator when the\n63: bare form would read differently (an argument starting with `-`, `.`, or `[`, a compound\n64: argument, or a call spread across lines, since an argument must start on the same line as its\n65: function). Both work anywhere an atom does, including as an operand. `select` and `map` are not\n66: special syntax -- they are ordinary names reached through this same rule.\n67: _Avoid_: call, invocation, function call\n68: \n69: ### Reaching into a value\n70: \n71: **Spec**:\n72: What an access says about one dimension: keep it, narrow it, or collapse it. Every dimension of a\n73: value needs one, which is why `[]` is written rather than assumed.\n74: \n75: **Keep**:\n76: A spec that leaves a dimension at full extent, written `[]`. Streamable, since it consumes\n77: nothing.\n78: \n79: **Narrow**:\n80: A spec that leaves a dimension at reduced extent, such as a mask. Streamable.\n81: \n82: **Collapse**:\n83: A spec that removes a dimension, such as an index. Not streamable: it has to consume to find the\n84: entry, so on a stream it destroys what it passed.\n85: \n86: **Selection**:\n87: Choosing along a dimension -- which entries. `select` and index specs are selections.\n88: _Avoid_: filter, projection\n89: \n90: **Projection**:\n91: Choosing a field out of a record -- which part of each entry. `.name` is a projection.\n92: _Avoid_: select, field access, column selection\n93: \n94: ### Guarantees\n95: \n96: **Rectangular**:\n97: Every entry of a dimension has the same extent in the next dimension down. A refinement, not a\n98: requirement: it is what makes collapsing an inner dimension total, so a non-rectangular value\n99: yields `Opt` where entries are missing rather than being inaccessible.\n100: _Avoid_: dense, uniform, matrix-shaped\n101: \n102: **Ragged**:\n103: Not rectangular. Dimensions are still enumerable and still addressable; only totality is lost.\n104: _Avoid_: jagged, irregular\n105: \n106: **Enumerable dimensions**:\n107: A type fixes how deep it goes, so its dimensions can be named in order. `Vec<Vec<Int>>` has two.\n108: A type of unbounded depth has none, and is reached only by recursive descent.\n109: \n110: ### Choosing among shapes\n111: \n112: **Enum**:\n113: A declared, closed set of named variants, nominal: the name is the identity, and consuming one\n114: must handle every variant. As data it is plain JSON, never an opaque value.\n115: _Avoid_: union, sum type, ADT, tagged union\n116: \n117: **Variant**:\n118: One named alternative of an enum, optionally carrying a payload of one type -- and itself a\n119: type, a subtype of its enum, so a signature can name it. Capitalized, per the casing rule,\n120: because it is a type. As data: the single-key wrapper, `{\"Circle\": {\"r\": 1}}`, the name\n121: verbatim.\n122: _Avoid_: case, constructor, alternative\n123: \n124: **Unit variant**:\n125: A variant with no payload. As data: a bare string, the name verbatim (`\"Active\"`). Wild\n126: lowercase string enums are no longer directly typeable; they wait for the codec layer.\n127: _Avoid_: nullary constructor, bare tag\n128: \n129: ### Where multiplicity lives\n130: \n131: **Stream**:\n132: The type of effect-layer multiplicity: `Stream<T>` says an expression yields its entries one at\n133: a time as evaluation proceeds, not that a stream object exists. Born at a source, consumed\n134: exactly once, dead at `collect` or a sink; never inside a record, a `Vec`, or another `Stream`.\n135: _Avoid_: lazy list, generator, iterator, channel\n136: \n137: **Source**:\n138: An expression a `Stream` is born from, and the only way one arises: `inputs`, `lines`.\n139: _Avoid_: producer, reader\n140: \n141: **Sink**:\n142: A stream consumer legal only as a program's outermost expression, writing as it goes, with no\n143: result type. `jsonlines` is one.\n144: _Avoid_: printer, writer, output function\n145: \n146: **Collect**:\n147: The named spelling of reify at the stream boundary: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`, the one explicit way\n148: a `Stream` becomes a value. Not a third layer shifter.\n149: _Avoid_: materialize, realize, to_vec\n\n(End of file - total 149 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# toylang\n\nA compiled, statically typed language for transforming data, taking jq as its main inspiration\nwithout aiming at compatibility with it. This file is the glossary and nothing else: what the\nterms mean, not how anything is built. The design lives in `draft.md`, the build order in\n`plans/`, and what building it taught us in `research-log/`.\n\n## Language\n\n### Shape of a value\n\n**Scalar**:\nA value with no interior to address: `Int`, `Str`, `Bool`.\n\n**Record**:\nA fixed set of differently-typed parts addressed by name, where the names are part of the type.\nType theory calls this a product, which is exact and says nothing to a reader who has not met the\nword, so that name is not used here.\n_Avoid_: object, struct, row, product\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/CONTEXT.md","text":"# toylang\n\nA compiled, statically typed language for transforming data, taking jq as its main inspiration\nwithout aiming at compatibility with it. This file is the glossary and nothing else: what the\nterms mean, not how anything is built. The design lives in `draft.md`, the build order in\n`plans/`, and what building it taught us in `research-log/`.\n\n## Language\n\n### Shape of a value\n\n**Scalar**:\nA value with no interior to address: `Int`, `Str`, `Bool`.\n\n**Record**:\nA fixed set of differently-typed parts addressed by name, where the names are part of the type.\nType theory calls this a product, which is exact and says nothing to a reader who has not met the\nword, so that name is not used here.\n_Avoid_: object, struct, row, product\n\n**Dimension**:\nAn axis along which a value repeats, addressed by position, whose entries all have the same type.\nA `Vec` has one; a tensor has several. A type's dimensions are fixed by the type, in order.\n_Avoid_: axis, rank, column\n\n**Extent**:\nHow many entries a dimension has. A number, not a type.\n_Avoid_: size, cardinality\n\n**Field**:\nOne named part of a record. `name` is a field of `{name: Str, age: Int}`.\n_Avoid_: column, attribute, component\n\n**Map**:\nA collection whose keys are data rather than type-level, with one value type. Distinct from a\nrecord: a record's keys are known to the compiler, a map's are known only to the program.\n_Avoid_: object, dict, record\n\n**Name casing**:\nA name beginning with a capital letter is a type; a name beginning with a lowercase letter is a\nvalue, which covers functions and their parameters. Field names are exempt, because they come\nfrom data and a JSON object is entitled to a key spelled `Name`.\n\n### Building a value\n\n**Record literal**:\nThe form that builds a record from its fields, and the inverse of a projection.\n`{name: .n, age: .a}` is one. Its type is the names and types of its fields, so it answers what\nit is from its contents alone and never needs its position to say. In argument position it is\nalso how a function takes more than one thing, because a function takes one argument and a record\nis how several travel as one.\n_Avoid_: object literal, struct literal, product literal, construction\n\n**Vec literal**:\nThe form that builds a dimension from its entries. `[1, 2]` is one. Unlike a record literal it\ncannot always answer what it is, because an entry is where an element type comes from and an\nempty one has none.\n_Avoid_: array literal, list literal\n\n**Application**:\nCalling a function with its one argument. Two spellings, one meaning: `f x`, the bare form, is\nthe default, and `f(x)` is the same call with the argument grouped -- the disambiguator when the\nbare form would read differently (an argument starting with `-`, `.`, or `[`, a compound\nargument, or a call spread across lines, since an argument must start on the same line as its\nfunction). Both work anywhere an atom does, including as an operand. `select` and `map` are not\nspecial syntax -- they are ordinary names reached through this same rule.\n_Avoid_: call, invocation, function call\n\n### Reaching into a value\n\n**Spec**:\nWhat an access says about one dimension: keep it, narrow it, or collapse it. Every dimension of a\nvalue needs one, which is why `[]` is written rather than assumed.\n\n**Keep**:\nA spec that leaves a dimension at full extent, written `[]`. Streamable, since it consumes\nnothing.\n\n**Narrow**:\nA spec that leaves a dimension at reduced extent, such as a mask. Streamable.\n\n**Collapse**:\nA spec that removes a dimension, such as an index. Not streamable: it has to consume to find the\nentry, so on a stream it destroys what it passed.\n\n**Selection**:\nChoosing along a dimension -- which entries. `select` and index specs are selections.\n_Avoid_: filter, projection\n\n**Projection**:\nChoosing a field out of a record -- which part of each entry. `.name` is a projection.\n_Avoid_: select, field access, column selection\n\n### Guarantees\n\n**Rectangular**:\nEvery entry of a dimension has the same extent in the next dimension down. A refinement, not a\nrequirement: it is what makes collapsing an inner dimension total, so a non-rectangular value\nyields `Opt` where entries are missing rather than being inaccessible.\n_Avoid_: dense, uniform, matrix-shaped\n\n**Ragged**:\nNot rectangular. Dimensions are still enumerable and still addressable; only totality is lost.\n_Avoid_: jagged, irregular\n\n**Enumerable dimensions**:\nA type fixes how deep it goes, so its dimensions can be named in order. `Vec<Vec<Int>>` has two.\nA type of unbounded depth has none, and is reached only by recursive descent.\n\n### Choosing among shapes\n\n**Enum**:\nA declared, closed set of named variants, nominal: the name is the identity, and consuming one\nmust handle every variant. As data it is plain JSON, never an opaque value.\n_Avoid_: union, sum type, ADT, tagged union\n\n**Variant**:\nOne named alternative of an enum, optionally carrying a payload of one type -- and itself a\ntype, a subtype of its enum, so a signature can name it. Capitalized, per the casing rule,\nbecause it is a type. As data: the single-key wrapper, `{\"Circle\": {\"r\": 1}}`, the name\nverbatim.\n_Avoid_: case, constructor, alternative\n\n**Unit variant**:\nA variant with no payload. As data: a bare string, the name verbatim (`\"Active\"`). Wild\nlowercase string enums are no longer directly typeable; they wait for the codec layer.\n_Avoid_: nullary constructor, bare tag\n\n### Where multiplicity lives\n\n**Stream**:\nThe type of effect-layer multiplicity: `Stream<T>` says an expression yields its entries one at\na time as evaluation proceeds, not that a stream object exists. Born at a source, consumed\nexactly once, dead at `collect` or a sink; never inside a record, a `Vec`, or another `Stream`.\n_Avoid_: lazy list, generator, iterator, channel\n\n**Source**:\nAn expression a `Stream` is born from, and the only way one arises: `inputs`, `lines`.\n_Avoid_: producer, reader\n\n**Sink**:\nA stream consumer legal only as a program's outermost expression, writing as it goes, with no\nresult type. `jsonlines` is one.\n_Avoid_: printer, writer, output function\n\n**Collect**:\nThe named spelling of reify at the stream boundary: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`, the one explicit way\na `Stream` becomes a value. Not a third layer shifter.\n_Avoid_: materialize, realize, to_vec","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":149,"totalLines":149,"truncated":false}},"title":"CONTEXT.md","time":{"start":1788387203020,"end":1788387203026}},"id":"prt_0642f16a9001b1gqJc2EtZVIPU","sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","messageID":"msg_0642f052a00187qrNcV5erqXLR"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788387203427,"sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-ab8dc4af51412cd8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/README.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # toylang\n2: \n3: A compiled, statically typed jq dialect. JSON is the native value model, not a library, and\n4: jq's semantics stay the reference while the language extends and generalizes them: Rust-like\n5: syntax, records and enums in the type system, and a compiler that emits real code instead of\n6: walking an AST.\n7: \n8: The ambition is general-purpose, but through a deliberate sequence. The beachhead is CLI data\n9: transformation, where the dogfood test is concrete: it replaces jq in its author's own shell.\n10: \n11: **Status: exploratory.** No releases, no stability promise. This page shows what runs today;\n12: the design record is [draft.md](draft.md), and what is still open is tracked in\n13: [plans/questions.md](plans/questions.md).\n14: \n15: ## What it looks like\n16: \n17: [`examples/adults.toy`](examples/adults.toy) reads a user list on stdin and prints the names\n18: of the adults:\n19: \n20: ```\n21: fn adults(db: {users: Vec<{name: Str, age: Int}>}) -> Vec<Str> =\n22:     db.users | select(.age >= 18) | .[].name\n23: \n24: adults(input)\n25: ```\n26: \n27: ```\n28: $ echo '{\"users\":[{\"name\":\"ada\",\"age\":36},{\"name\":\"tim\",\"age\":12},{\"name\":\"grace\",\"age\":85}]}' | cargo run --quiet -- run examples/adults.toy\n29: [\"ada\",\"grace\"]\n30: ```\n31: \n32: The pipeline, `select`, and `.name` are jq's; the typed signature and the record type it\n33: declares are not. `.[]` is written rather than assumed because crossing a dimension is a\n34: boundary, and the language does not erase boundaries.\n35: \n36: ## Enums\n37: \n38: Enums are the first deliberate import from Rust: a declared, closed set of variants, and a\n39: match that must handle every one. As data an enum is plain JSON, never an opaque value\n40: ([ADR 0009](docs/adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)): a unit variant is a\n41: bare string, a payload variant a single-key wrapper like `{\"circle\":{\"r\":1}}`, so an enum\n42: types wire data directly.\n43: \n44: [`examples/shapes.toy`](examples/shapes.toy):\n45: \n46: ```\n47: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n48: \n49: fn area_ish(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r or point -> 0\n50: \n51: {a: area_ish(Shape.point), b: area_ish(circle{r: 3})}\n52: ```\n53: \n54: ```\n55: $ cargo run --quiet -- run examples/shapes.toy\n56: {\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n57: ```\n58: \n59: Match arms chain with `or`; the first that matches wins. The match is closed-world: a\n60: program whose match handles only `circle`,\n61: \n62: ```\n63: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n64: \n65: fn area_ish(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r\n66: \n67: area_ish(Shape.point)\n68: ```\n69: \n70: is refused:\n71: \n72: ```\n73: a match over `Shape` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `point` (at byte 73)\n74: ```\n75: \n76: ## Seven backends, kept as falsifiers\n77: \n78: The same program compiles to Lua, JavaScript, jq, Go, Python, Rust, and native code through\n79: LLVM:\n80: \n81: ```\n82: $ for b in lua js jq go py rust llvm; do cargo run --quiet -- run examples/shapes.toy $b; done\n83: {\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n84: {\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n85: {\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n86: {\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n87: {\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n88: {\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n89: {\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n90: ```\n91: \n92: Seven targets are not a compatibility promise, and not all of them will be kept. Each was\n93: admitted because it is structurally unlike the others and therefore falsifies checker rules\n94: the rest satisfy by accident\n95: ([ADR 0002](docs/adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md)): jq immediately broke two programs\n96: that three imperative backends had agreed on, and Go's exact constant arithmetic refused an\n97: out-of-range `Int` literal that four backends had printed unwrapped. The test suite enforces\n98: the agreement: every corpus case runs on every backend, and either they all print the same\n99: output or they all refuse the program.\n100: \n101: Each backend emits source (or LLVM IR) that the target's own toolchain runs\n102: ([ADR 0004](docs/adr/0004-backends-emit-source-run-by-real-toolchains.md)), so running a\n103: given backend needs that toolchain installed. Lua is the default.\n104: \n105: ## Where things live\n106: \n107: - [draft.md](draft.md): the design record. Long on purpose; it is the document of record, not\n108:   this page.\n109: - [plans/questions.md](plans/questions.md): the numbered design questions, open and settled.\n110: - [CONTEXT.md](CONTEXT.md): the glossary. Words like record, dimension, and projection are\n111:   used precisely, and this is where they are pinned.\n112: - [docs/adr/](docs/adr): decisions, one file each.\n113: - [tests/corpus/](tests/corpus): one YAML file per case, run on every backend; the corpus is\n114:   the spec ([ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-the-corpus-is-the-spec.md)).\n115: - [research-log/](research-log): what building it taught, one finding per file.\n116: - [plans/](plans): build order for the piece currently underway.\n117: \n118: ## Running it\n119: \n120: `cargo build` produces the compiler; `just test` runs the suite (it needs `cargo-nextest`\n121: and the backend toolchains). `toylang run FILE [backend]` compiles and runs a program,\n122: `toylang emit FILE backend` prints the emitted source, and `toylang build FILE` links a\n123: standalone native binary.\n124: \n125: `toylang fmt` is the formatter, in two forms. Given a file it prints that file formatted and\n126: changes nothing on disk. Given nothing it walks down from the current folder instead, listing\n127: every `.toy` file that is not in canonical form and exiting nonzero if any is; `toylang fmt\n128: --write` makes the same walk and rewrites them, exiting nonzero if it changed anything. Either\n129: form of the walk can gate a commit.\n\n(End of file - total 129 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# toylang\n\nA compiled, statically typed jq dialect. JSON is the native value model, not a library, and\njq's semantics stay the reference while the language extends and generalizes them: Rust-like\nsyntax, records and enums in the type system, and a compiler that emits real code instead of\nwalking an AST.\n\nThe ambition is general-purpose, but through a deliberate sequence. The beachhead is CLI data\ntransformation, where the dogfood test is concrete: it replaces jq in its author's own shell.\n\n**Status: exploratory.** No releases, no stability promise. This page shows what runs today;\nthe design record is [draft.md](draft.md), and what is still open is tracked in\n[plans/questions.md](plans/questions.md).\n\n## What it looks like\n\n[`examples/adults.toy`](examples/adults.toy) reads a user list on stdin and prints the names\nof the adults:\n\n```","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-152/README.md","text":"# toylang\n\nA compiled, statically typed jq dialect. JSON is the native value model, not a library, and\njq's semantics stay the reference while the language extends and generalizes them: Rust-like\nsyntax, records and enums in the type system, and a compiler that emits real code instead of\nwalking an AST.\n\nThe ambition is general-purpose, but through a deliberate sequence. The beachhead is CLI data\ntransformation, where the dogfood test is concrete: it replaces jq in its author's own shell.\n\n**Status: exploratory.** No releases, no stability promise. This page shows what runs today;\nthe design record is [draft.md](draft.md), and what is still open is tracked in\n[plans/questions.md](plans/questions.md).\n\n## What it looks like\n\n[`examples/adults.toy`](examples/adults.toy) reads a user list on stdin and prints the names\nof the adults:\n\n```\nfn adults(db: {users: Vec<{name: Str, age: Int}>}) -> Vec<Str> =\n    db.users | select(.age >= 18) | .[].name\n\nadults(input)\n```\n\n```\n$ echo '{\"users\":[{\"name\":\"ada\",\"age\":36},{\"name\":\"tim\",\"age\":12},{\"name\":\"grace\",\"age\":85}]}' | cargo run --quiet -- run examples/adults.toy\n[\"ada\",\"grace\"]\n```\n\nThe pipeline, `select`, and `.name` are jq's; the typed signature and the record type it\ndeclares are not. `.[]` is written rather than assumed because crossing a dimension is a\nboundary, and the language does not erase boundaries.\n\n## Enums\n\nEnums are the first deliberate import from Rust: a declared, closed set of variants, and a\nmatch that must handle every one. As data an enum is plain JSON, never an opaque value\n([ADR 0009](docs/adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)): a unit variant is a\nbare string, a payload variant a single-key wrapper like `{\"circle\":{\"r\":1}}`, so an enum\ntypes wire data directly.\n\n[`examples/shapes.toy`](examples/shapes.toy):\n\n```\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n\nfn area_ish(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r or point -> 0\n\n{a: area_ish(Shape.point), b: area_ish(circle{r: 3})}\n```\n\n```\n$ cargo run --quiet -- run examples/shapes.toy\n{\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n```\n\nMatch arms chain with `or`; the first that matches wins. The match is closed-world: a\nprogram whose match handles only `circle`,\n\n```\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n\nfn area_ish(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r\n\narea_ish(Shape.point)\n```\n\nis refused:\n\n```\na match over `Shape` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `point` (at byte 73)\n```\n\n## Seven backends, kept as falsifiers\n\nThe same program compiles to Lua, JavaScript, jq, Go, Python, Rust, and native code through\nLLVM:\n\n```\n$ for b in lua js jq go py rust llvm; do cargo run --quiet -- run examples/shapes.toy $b; done\n{\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n{\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n{\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n{\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n{\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n{\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n{\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n```\n\nSeven targets are not a compatibility promise, and not all of them will be kept. Each was\nadmitted because it is structurally unlike the others and therefore falsifies checker rules\nthe rest satisfy by accident\n([ADR 0002](docs/adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md)): jq immediately broke two programs\nthat three imperative backends had agreed on, and Go's exact constant arithmetic refused an\nout-of-range `Int` literal that four backends had printed unwrapped. The test suite enforces\nthe agreement: every corpus case runs on every backend, and either they all print the same\noutput or they all refuse the program.\n\nEach backend emits source (or LLVM IR) that the target's own toolchain runs\n([ADR 0004](docs/adr/0004-backends-emit-source-run-by-real-toolchains.md)), so running a\ngiven backend needs that toolchain installed. Lua is the default.\n\n## Where things live\n\n- [draft.md](draft.md): the design record. Long on purpose; it is the document of record, not\n  this page.\n- [plans/questions.md](plans/questions.md): the numbered design questions, open and settled.\n- [CONTEXT.md](CONTEXT.md): the glossary. Words like record, dimension, and projection are\n  used precisely, and this is where they are pinned.\n- [docs/adr/](docs/adr): decisions, one file each.\n- [tests/corpus/](tests/corpus): one YAML file per case, run on every backend; the corpus is\n  the spec ([ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-the-corpus-is-the-spec.md)).\n- [research-log/](research-log): what building it taught, one finding per file.\n- [plans/](plans): build order for the piece currently underway.\n\n## Running it\n\n`cargo build` produces the compiler; `just test` runs the suite (it needs `cargo-nextest`\nand the backend toolchains). `toylang run FILE [backend]` compiles and runs a program,\n`toylang emit FILE backend` prints the emitted source, and `toylang build FILE` links a\nstandalone native binary.\n\n`toylang fmt` is the formatter, in two forms. Given a file it prints that file formatted and\nchanges nothing on disk. Given nothing it walks down from the current folder instead, listing\nevery `.toy` file that is not in canonical form and exiting nonzero if any is; `toylang fmt\n--write` makes the same walk and rewrites them, exiting nonzero if it changed anything. Either\nform of the walk can gate a commit.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":129,"totalLines":129,"truncated":false}},"title":"README.md","time":{"start":1788387203421,"end":1788387203425}},"id":"prt_0642f17ce001k54BsY0p3acGBe","sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","messageID":"msg_0642f052a00187qrNcV5erqXLR"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788387203437,"sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","part":{"id":"prt_0642f19690018omPIUIw0xXQFx","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"09ace4a8e7c6b4501452a7c5045e43102f155e47","messageID":"msg_0642f052a00187qrNcV5erqXLR","sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":31667,"input":8515,"output":142,"reasoning":226,"cache":{"write":0,"read":22784}},"cost":0.000984259}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788387204397,"sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","part":{"id":"prt_0642f1d2a001g84TbSrUWXNP8U","messageID":"msg_0642f1976001iuYYjfgHGNc74q","sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","snapshot":"09ace4a8e7c6b4501452a7c5045e43102f155e47","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788387235384,"sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a9150fe670bda3a8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show 83065f1 --stat"},"output":"commit 83065f1956128e80c02eac7001944b64ed148604\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 22:56:39 2026 +0200\n\n    Apply round-5 ruling for function-signature-matching-syntax: pick A\n    \n    Maintainer picked call-form hoist (A) over the thesis-favored C, on the\n    grounds that \"doesn't parse yet\" isn't disqualifying this early in the\n    language's design. Converts the row from decide to build; gh:152 closes\n    on landing.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n plans/board.yaml | 6 +++---\n 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)\n","metadata":{"output":"commit 83065f1956128e80c02eac7001944b64ed148604\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 22:56:39 2026 +0200\n\n    Apply round-5 ruling for function-signature-matching-syntax: pick A\n    \n    Maintainer picked call-form hoist (A) over the thesis-favored C, on the\n    grounds that \"doesn't parse yet\" isn't disqualifying this early in the\n    language's design. Converts the row from decide to build; gh:152 closes\n    on landing.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n plans/board.yaml | 6 +++---\n 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git show 83065f1 --stat","time":{"start":1788387235382,"end":1788387235383}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the language better. Let me look at the board row again:\n\n\"Round 5 RULED: maintainer picked A -- call-form hoist (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or Quit -> ...)), against the round-5 thesis which favored C on parse-today grounds. Maintainer note: \"we are building a new language and it's in the early stages; most feature requests do not assume this will already parse and that is natural\" -- i.e. A's three novel surfaces (new fn-declaration shape, return-type inference, type-name-as-match-call expression form) are accepted design cost, not disqualifying. Build task: implement A. gh:152 to be closed on landing.\"\n\nSo my task is to implement option A: call-form hoist. The example: `fn render = Msg(Ping -> \"*ping*\" or Quit -> \"*quit*\" or Text -> .body)`.\n\nLet me understand the current syntax. Currently:\n- `fn area_ish(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r or point -> 0`\n\nOption A would be:\n- `fn render = Msg(Ping -> \"*ping*\" or Quit -> \"*quit*\" or Text -> .body)`\n\nWait, but this changes multiple things:\n1. New fn-declaration shape: `fn name = <match expression>` where the parameter is dropped entirely (`. `is always the input)\n2. Return-type inference: no explicit `-> Int`\n3. Type-name-as-match-call expression form: `Msg(...)` where `Msg` is a type name used as a function-like match call\n\nHmm, this is a large feature. Let me look at how the language currently works. Let me look at the git log more carefully. The commit `83065f1 Apply round-5 ruling for function-signature-matching-syntax: pick A` - what did it do? Let me check what that commit did and where it is relative to my branch.\n\nActually wait - let me re-check. My branch is issue-152, at fd6284c, behind origin/main by 1 commit. The git log shows commits including `83065f1 Apply round-5 ruling for function-signature-matching-syntax: pick A`. Let me check if that's on my branch or in the log because of some merge. Let me check where 83065f1 is.\n\nActually the log I saw was `git log --oneline -20` from HEAD which is fd6284c. So 83065f1 is an ancestor of my current HEAD. Hmm, but that's weird - the round-5 ruling was \"applied\" already. What did that commit do?\n\nLet me look at the diff of that commit and understand what \"apply round-5 ruling\" means here.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0642f81a10016Ztza4LeU65VM5","sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","messageID":"msg_0642f1976001iuYYjfgHGNc74q"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788387241002,"sessionID":"ses_f9bd1f9e7ffe9fbmbl05uGvnlZ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8487145129425dac","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show 83065f1"},"output":"commit 83065f1956128e80c02eac7001944b64ed148604\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 22:56:39 2026 +0200\n\n    Apply round-5 ruling for function-signature-matching-syntax: pick A\n    \n    Maintainer picked call-form hoist (A) over the thesis-favored C, on the\n    grounds that \"doesn't parse yet\" isn't disqualifying this early in the\n    language's design. Converts the row from decide to build; gh:152 closes\n    on landing.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\ndiff --git a/plans/board.yaml b/plans/board.yaml\nindex 7bde206..231ad46 100644\n--- a/plans/board.yaml\n+++ b/plans/board.yaml\n@@ -6,9 +6,9 @@\n   title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-174 (no activity 0h, 1 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-174-20260902/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\n - id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n   issue: gh:152\n-  title: 'Grill the concrete spelling for implicit-. params via matching syntax -- round 1 couldn''t pick without more context; round 2 presents the maintainer''s own hoist-into-signature sketch (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or ...)) as a concrete candidate (gh:152). Round 2 answer: no pick yet; round 3 stress-tested each of the three candidates (A/B/C) against the real parser and re-asked. Round 3 answer: still no pick, needs signature-matching-deeper-research first; round 4 presented that research (all six type shapes) with full code probes per option. Round 4 answer: still no pick, and it is process feedback, not a content signal -- option cards were too long (\"at least 8 times shorter\"), code belongs once in the thesis not repeated per option, and rounds should frame how each option interacts with future plans rather than asking for an early commit. grill-via-annotations/SKILL.md''s option-card rule updated to match (2026-09-02). Round 5 must re-ask the same A/B/C content (A and B still fail identically pre-shape, C still the only candidate that parses all six shapes) in the new short-card, future-framed shape -- no new research needed.'\n-  kind: decide\n-  needs: [signature-matching-deeper-research]\n+  title: 'Round 5 RULED: maintainer picked A -- call-form hoist (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or Quit -> ...)), against the round-5 thesis which favored C on parse-today grounds. Maintainer note: \"we are building a new language and it''s in the early stages; most feature requests do not assume this will already parse and that is natural\" -- i.e. A''s three novel surfaces (new fn-declaration shape, return-type inference, type-name-as-match-call expression form) are accepted design cost, not disqualifying. Build task: implement A. gh:152 to be closed on landing.'\n+  kind: build\n+  needs: []\n   prio: 3\n   status: todo\n \n","metadata":{"output":"commit 83065f1956128e80c02eac7001944b64ed148604\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 22:56:39 2026 +0200\n\n    Apply round-5 ruling for function-signature-matching-syntax: pick A\n    \n    Maintainer picked call-form hoist (A) over the thesis-favored C, on the\n    grounds that \"doesn't parse yet\" isn't disqualifying this early in the\n    language's design. Converts the row from decide to build; gh:152 closes\n    on landing.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\ndiff --git a/plans/board.yaml b/plans/board.yaml\nindex 7bde206..231ad46 100644\n--- a/plans/board.yaml\n+++ b/plans/board.yaml\n@@ -6,9 +6,9 @@\n   title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-174 (no activity 0h, 1 run(s)